e a  strong and fully functioning and fully  staffed State Department although this  is an advertisement for the Treasury  Department we're all former Treasury  officials the work that we've been able  to do in countering terrorist financing  not to mention many many other areas is  enormous ly dependent on having a state  department that is out there fully  empowered and fully functioning so I can  applaud so just a couple of quick  thoughts picking up on what one was  saying the the effort to combat terror  is financing I came to the Treasury  Department in 2009 at a point where one  and and his his team with Stuart Levey  and others at Treasury had made enormous  amount of progress in combating that  what was post 9/11 the principle problem  which was funding coming out of Saudi  Arabia Stewart famously said if he could  snap his fingers and and there was  financing in Saudi Arabia that would  make all the difference or something  along those lines that actually did not  make him so popular among the with the  ambassador in Saudi at the time but but  by the time I got there in 2009 the  Saudis had shifted quite significantly  in their perspective on this issue and  in their willingness and ability to work  with the US I then had the opportunities  to try to build on on those efforts what  we did in my my ten-year Treasury which  spanned from oh nine to fifteen was  principally to work bilaterally and I  think this bears on the question of how  important it is on the further to be a  unified GCC  by and large with some very minor  exceptions all of the efforts that we  undertook were bilateral with the Saudis  with the Emiratis with the Bahrainis  with the Cadiz and the cutter ease the  sharing of intelligence information  which is critical to be able to identify  who the terrorists financiers are how  they're raising their money how they're  moving their money that also tended to  be I think I would exclusively as  probably the right word they're  exclusively bilateral the the only  exception in my recollection was we made  some efforts to enlist the Saudis to  work with us in sort of a trilateral  fashion with the Kuwait ease and the  cutter ease and remember a couple of  occasions in particular were we would be  in Saudi speaking with Mohammed bin  Nayef or Abu Ali the folks who were sort  of most important in the finance in the  interior ministry there on the terrorist  financing issue to try to get them to  essentially to put pressure with us on  the cutter ease and the Kuwait ease that  did not work so well to not put too fine  a point on it and I and I think was for  a variety of reasons but working in a  trilateral fashion didn't work and  working in the GCC more more broadly was  not something that we really try to try  to pursue so I so the current situation  in the Gulf right now where you see I  think a real potential for the GCC to  splinter and for Qatar to go its way and  the Saudis and the Emiratis their way  and with the other states sort of  falling in line I think that itself  isn't the problem I think the bigger  problem is that you have Carter which  had been making some progress in recent  years in prosecuting terrorists fanciers  in being a little bit more  forward-leaning in in its efforts no  longer feeling that it's in the club  inside the tent and we lose a little bit  of the sort of moral suasion that comes  from a combined effort of folks in the  Gulf and so that's that's what I'm  worried about in the in the current  situation is that you the progress that  we have seen with the countries in the  last couple of years which has been sort  of hard-won lots of efforts from  Treasury and from State and others to  move the countries along that that will  begin to to recede and then I'll just  make one final point we can pick up on  it later the other thing that you all  know happened recently in the Gulf that  I think has significant implications for  the future of counter-terrorist  financing is the